    {
      "id": "hipFcy69-x0qJKdlsuesA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2277.0441273631823,
      "y": -60.45463583617544,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aL",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1711978324,
      "version": 21,
      "versionNonce": 2122653804,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793003349640,
      "created": 1793003255442,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Packing assignment\nWhen an order is confirmed:\n\n1. Find active packers assigned to that dark store.\n2. Exclude packers who are unavailable or at their task limit.\n3. Select the eligible packer with the fewest active tasks.\n4. Assign the packing task in a database transaction.\n5. If no packer is available, keep the task in a pending queue.\n\n\nDelivery assignment\nWhen an order becomes ready for pickup:\n\n1. Find active drivers available for that store or its delivery zone.\n2. Exclude drivers who already have an active delivery or are at capacity.\n3. Select the closest eligible driver, or the least-loaded driver if you aren't\nmodelling locations.\n4. Atomically assign the delivery.\n5. If no driver is available, leave the delivery pending and retry later.\n\n\n3. Why not assign tasks directly inside the order API?\nYou could do everything inside OrderService, but that creates unnecessary\ncoupling.\n\nA cleaner structure is:\n- OrderService confirms the order.\n- PackingAssignmentService assigns the packing task.\n- FulfillmentService handles picking and packing progress.\n- DeliveryAssignmentService assigns drivers.\n- DeliveryService handles pickup and delivery.\n\n\n4. The concurrency problem that makes this interesting\nSuppose two orders are ready and only one driver is available.\n\nBoth requests might select the same driver if you merely query available = true\nand then update the assignment afterward.\n\nPrevent this by making assignment atomic—for example, locking the driver's\ndatabase row and checking availability inside the transaction, or using a\nconditional database update. Add a database constraint to prevent duplicate\nactive assignments where appropriate.\n\nThe same principle applies to packers.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Packing assignment\nWhen an order is confirmed:\n\n1. Find active packers assigned to that dark store.\n2. Exclude packers who are unavailable or at their task limit.\n3. Select the eligible packer with the fewest active tasks.\n4. Assign the packing task in a database transaction.\n5. If no packer is available, keep the task in a pending queue.\n\n\nDelivery assignment\nWhen an order becomes ready for pickup:\n\n1. Find active drivers available for that store or its delivery zone.\n2. Exclude drivers who already have an active delivery or are at capacity.\n3. Select the closest eligible driver, or the least-loaded driver if you aren't modelling locations.\n4. Atomically assign the delivery.\n5. If no driver is available, leave the delivery pending and retry later.\n\n\n3. Why not assign tasks directly inside the order API?\nYou could do everything inside OrderService, but that creates unnecessary coupling.\n\nA cleaner structure is:\n- OrderService confirms the order.\n- PackingAssignmentService assigns the packing task.\n- FulfillmentService handles picking and packing progress.\n- DeliveryAssignmentService assigns drivers.\n- DeliveryService handles pickup and delivery.\n\n\n4. The concurrency problem that makes this interesting\nSuppose two orders are ready and only one driver is available.\n\nBoth requests might select the same driver if you merely query available = true and then update the assignment afterward.\n\nPrevent this by making assignment atomic—for example, locking the driver's database row and checking availability inside the transaction, or using a conditional database update. Add a database constraint to prevent duplicate active assignments where appropriate.\n\nThe same principle applies to packers.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    }
  ],
